# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Drawer 27 Opens Between Pick and Pitch

**Story & Context:** The debut begins with one panduri note recorded in extreme detail. The pick touches the string, a small scrape appears, the string releases, the second fret clicks, and only then does the full pitch bloom. Levan places a wooden archive-drawer sound inside the few milliseconds of perceived space between attack and stable tone, not by literally time-stretching the pluck but by arranging the ensemble so the drawer becomes the next audible event.

At 98 BPM, Giorgi establishes a 3+2+2 cycle with only two doli strokes per bar. Mariam answers the panduri pitch bloom with one thread-damped dulcimer fourth. Elene enters after the hand mute with a low breath-only recorder attack. The first half of the track repeats the same tonal center while changing which stage of the Granule Chain receives an answer.

Midway through, Nino switches from a soft plectrum to fingernail attack. The pitch material stays unchanged, but the stronger transient causes the ensemble to shorten every following silence. The arrangement becomes denser without adding instruments. The final phrase ends after the fret click; the expected pitch is deliberately muted before it blooms.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close documentary image of a wooden flat-file drawer labeled 27 half-open beside a dark panduri, a single string and fret in macro focus, burgundy thread sample card and graphite timing marks on cream paper. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “drawer twenty-seven,” “pick first,” “pitch later,” and “close before the note.”

## Track 2: Burgundy Thread Shortens the Fifth

**Story & Context:** Mariam prepares one dulcimer course with a strip of burgundy thread so its fifth decays far faster than the neighboring strings. The track is built around alternating long and short versions of the same interval. Nino mirrors this on panduri by allowing one open fifth to ring and immediately palm-muting the next.

The pulse alternates 5/8 and 7/8 at 104 BPM. Giorgi does not mark every meter change; instead, his doli enters only when both string instruments produce the short version of the fifth in the same cycle. Elene plays two low recorder notes separated by a key-click figure, creating a breath counterpart to the long/short string contrast.

Levan uses only three archive sounds: thread reel rotation, a metal drawer label tapped by fingernail, and paper scissors closing once. Each appears at a different structural boundary and is never looped. In the final section, Mariam removes the burgundy thread preparation while the track is still running. The previously short dulcimer fifth suddenly rings into the room, becoming the longest sound on the record.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Burgundy thread woven between two hammered-dulcimer strings, one damped string beside one freely ringing course, panduri fifth notated on a faded textile pattern card, brass archive label catching cold fluorescent light. Spoken fragments limited to “burgundy thread,” “short fifth,” “leave the neighbor long,” and “remove it now.”

## Track 3: The Catalog Card Ends with a Hand Mute

**Story & Context:** The closer has no stable drum pulse for its first two minutes. Nino performs a sequence of isolated panduri notes separated by increasingly long silences. Each note corresponds to one box on an old five-field catalog card: preparation, attack, bloom, residue, mute. Levan places no environmental sound until the fifth field is reached.

Mariam uses soft mallets on unprepared dulcimer courses, producing the EP’s clearest metallic pitches. Elene sustains an underblown bass-recorder fundamental below selected notes but stops whenever the panduri is muted. Giorgi enters only once the card sequence repeats, playing a slow 2+2+3 doli figure at 94 BPM.

In the final passage, the ensemble gradually removes responses from the Granule Chain. First there is no doli after the attack, then no dulcimer after the bloom, then no recorder after the mute. Nino plays the final panduri note alone and stops it with the palm before its natural decay is complete. The last sound is the paper catalog card being turned face-down on the table.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Cream archival catalog card with five empty graphite boxes beside a small panduri, final box labeled by a hand-drawn mute symbol, soft dulcimer mallet and bass-recorder key in shallow focus, dark walnut table under one amber desk lamp. Spoken fragments limited to “five fields,” “leave the bloom,” “mute by hand,” and “turn the card over.”

